I wrote a function foo() that I want to terminate primaturely
in case it does not return within a given period of time.  I
thought I could kick off a pthread for foo() which is a bit of
a hassle since I have to pass the params of foo() as 
pthread_addr_t, however this is not the real problem.  After a
certain amount of time has elapsed I call pthread_cancel() to
terminate foo().
With this solution I have to adapt foo() - it now has to accept
exactly one parameter of pthread_addr_t.  What I would like was
to find a way to define macros so I could write:

BEGIN_MONITOR_TIMEOUT(seconds) 
{
  foo(param1, param2, param3, param4);
} END_MONITOR_TIMEOUT;

Does somebody have a clue on how this could be implemented?
Am I right in the assumption that one can not simply use 
exceptions to terminate a thread since you can not raise an 
exception to a specific thread in a straightforward manner.

Thank you,
  Matthias



