                                 April 26, 1995

      OSF Research Institute Advances 
          Operating System Design

New Microkernel Performance Results Offer 
Commercial Viability


CAMBRIDGE, MA, April 26, 1995 -- The Open Software 
Foundation Research Institute today announced 
current results of the operating system program 
that show microkernel performance is equal to or 
better than commercially available Unix systems.  
Unlike earlier comparisons, the results indicate 
acceptable industry performance levels for overhead 
associated with boundary crossings in a modular 
operating system.  

The program compared performance measurements of 
OSF's MK6 microkernel operating system and other 
Unix systems.  Performance measurements on HP-PA 
workstations show the RI's modular system is 
comparable to Hewlett-Packard's HP-UX operating 
system.  The research also compares performance on 
the Intel 486 PC and shows the Research Institute's 
system is equal to or better than commercially 
available Unix systems such as Solaris 2.1, SCO 
UNIX, Sys V R4.2 and Free BSD.  

"The Research Institute's operating system program 
is forging new methodologies that allow scalable, 
configurable and balanced responses to a wide range 
of computing needs, across a broad spectrum of 
hardware architectures," said Dr. Ira Goldstein, 
Executive Vice President and Chief Scientist of 
OSF.  "This program is a synthesis of leading edge 
work from many sources into a modular framework 
that achieves a unification of benefits such as 
real-time, high trust, scalability and fault 
tolerance which can be suitably tested for 
evaluation by industry." 

Industrial and defense computing requirements are 
the driving force behind the operating system 
design.  These capabilities arise from distributed 
and mission-critical applications.  After 
completion of the prototype work, many vendors and 
system integrators will be able to avoid 
redesigning the operating system for each new 
architecture.  They will be able to deliver a 
customized operating system from a common base.  

The Research Institute's code has been used as the 
basis for  operating systems on commercially 
available and scalable supercomputers such as 
Convex Exemplar and Intel Paragon.  IBM Corporation 
has utilized Research Institute code, architecture 
and designs for mass market use on two uniprocessor 
architectures, Intel's 486/Pentium and IBM's 
PowerPC.  The Navy's advanced Aegis demonstration 
program, based at the Naval Surface Warfare Center 
(NSWC), is using the Research Institute�s code on a 
cluster of Digital Alpha workstations.

The OSF Research Institute's operating system 
program achieves economic innovation by leveraging 
collaborative contributions from U.S. Government 
support, industry sponsorship and a synthesis of 
relevant university research results in operating 
systems.  Sponsors of the operating system program 
include Apple Computer Company, AT&T, Digital 
Equipment Corporation, Electronics and 
Telecommunications Research Institute (ETRI), 
Hewlett-Packard Company, Hitachi Limited, IBM 
Corporation, and Novell, Inc.  University 
contributors include Carnegie Mellon University, 
University of Utah, University of Arizona and the 
University of California at Berkeley. 

The Open Software Foundation delivers technology 
innovations in all areas of open systems, including 
interoperability, scalability, portability and 
useability.  OSF has created a coalition of 
worldwide vendors and users in industry, government 
and academia that leverage their economic 
investments by working together to provide the best 
open systems technology solutions for distributed 
computing environments.  Headquartered in 
Cambridge, MA, with offices in Brussels, Grenoble 
and Tokyo, OSF has more than 400 members worldwide.

