descriptor 0 has been closed before the assembler is (eventually) invoked,
the assembler will choke with a useless error such as:

    as1: Error: , line 0: Obsolete or corrupt binasm file: /tmp/ctmaaagAoa

As this only occurs when using gcc (and not DEC's cc) I suspect it has
something to do with gcc's default use of the -pipe switch under OSF.

I only discovered this (eventually) because gcc was being invoked from a
daemon which had fd=0,1,2 all closed. The problem did not occur on our Suns.

The program below demonstrates the problem - if invoked with any arguments
the assembler will fail.

